{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "261",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 60s presented with a 3-month history of intertrigo involving his groin that he had been treating with a combination of clotrimazole cream, 1%, and hydrocortisone cream, 1%. The condition improved, but he then developed bright red, burning and painful nodules around his scrotum. Medical history included prostate cancer, hypertension, anxiety disorder, hyperlipidemia, gastroesophageal reflux disease, and diverticulosis. Current medications included omeprazole, alprazolam, amlodipine besylate, atenolol, simvastatin, and valsartan.\n\nTwo symmetrical solitary erythematous nodules were present in the right and left inguinal folds adjacent to the scrotum.\n\n- Histopathological examination:     - Epidermal ulceration overlying a diffuse dermal proliferation composed of sheets of histiocytes with eosinophilic granular cytoplasm    - Intracytoplasmic Michaelis-Gutmann bodies present as basophilic, somewhat laminated intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies measuring 5-8 μm in diameter    - Bodies highlighted with iron (Prussian blue) and calcium (Von Kossa) stains    - Background population of many plasma cells and few neutrophils noted  - Tissue Culture:    - Fungus culture: Negative    - Atypical mycobacteria culture: Negative      - Bacterial culture: Positive for E. coli  - Images:    Image 1 Title: Clinical image of right inguinal fold    Image 1 Description: Well-demarcated erythematous solitary papule in the right inguinal fold    Image 2 Title: Excisional biopsy pathologic analysis    Image 2 Description: Ulcerated nodule with sheeted dermal proliferation and histiocytes with pink granular cytoplasm containing laminated slightly basophilic inclusions (Michaelis-Gutmann bodies) with background plasma cells and neutrophils    Image 3 Title: Special stain results    Image 3 Description: Prussian blue and Von Kossa stains highlighting Michaelis-Gutmann bodies",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Cutaneous Nodular Lesions with Systemic Disease Associations",
